1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,001
Ferry. Atomic swaps between shielded ZEC and Bitcoin.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,815
Two parties. Z holds shielded ZEC, B holds bitcoin. Nobody in between.

3
00:00:08,815 --> 00:00:14,815
B locks BTC first: a Taproot output that needs both keys, with a timelocked way back.

4
00:00:14,815 --> 00:00:20,815
Z locks ZEC, Ironwood to Ironwood, to an address whose spend key is split: z plus b.

5
00:00:20,815 --> 00:00:26,815
B signs the redeem as an adaptor signature. Only Z's secret, z, can complete it.

6
00:00:26,815 --> 00:00:31,815
Z completes it and claims the BTC. One key-path signature.

7
00:00:31,815 --> 00:00:37,096
That signature is the key. B subtracts its adaptor and recovers z.

8
00:00:37,096 --> 00:00:43,096
After a random delay, B sweeps the note into its own shielded wallet. Done.

9
00:00:43,096 --> 00:00:53,177
What did the chains see? Bitcoin: a Taproot output and a single-signature spend, like any payment. Zcash: encrypted actions. Nothing links them.

10
00:00:53,177 --> 00:00:56,779
Now, what if Z vanishes?

11
00:00:56,779 --> 00:01:02,779
Same start: B locks bitcoin, Z locks shielded ZEC.

12
00:01:02,779 --> 00:01:05,846
Then Z goes silent.

13
00:01:05,846 --> 00:01:13,846
After timelock T1, B cancels and takes its bitcoin back. The refund signature reveals b.

14
00:01:13,846 --> 00:01:20,846
Z reads b from the refund and sweeps its ZEC home. Nobody loses a coin.

15
00:01:20,846 --> 00:01:27,847
No custodian. ZEC never leaves the shielded pool. And when it succeeds, the Bitcoin side looks like any other payment.
